IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `scja-core`

-- scja3-sch7-seventh-schedule.l4: the Seventh Schedule (civil cases that the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal can hear with fewer than 3 Judges).
-- Layer 1, written from the text alone (SCJA1969.txt lines 6270-6481).  Answers seam 14 of PLAN.md; the asking sections are ss 36(1) and 54(1), in scja2's range.
-- The Schedule is data: one constructor of `sch 7 matter before the court` per item, then a look-up per paragraph.
-- A result is JUST (the panel), or NOTHING for a matter the Schedule does not list (ss 36(1), 54(1) say "if the Seventh Schedule so provides");
-- the yes/no twin answers JUST TRUE for a listed matter and JUST FALSE for an unlisted one in the two courts the Schedule speaks of.

§ `Seventh Schedule: cases where the civil jurisdiction of the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal can be exercised by fewer than 3 Judges`

-- The matters the Schedule names, in the Schedule's order.  Paragraphs 1 and 3 name the same first five matters for the two courts, and
-- paragraphs 2 and 4 name the same ten, so one constructor serves both; the paragraph is found by the court.
DECLARE `sch 7 matter before the court` IS ONE OF
    `sch 7 an application to record a judgment or order made by consent`
    `sch 7 an application for an order incidental to a judgment or order made by consent`
    `sch 7 an application to adduce further evidence in proceedings before the court`
    `sch 7 an application for costs, or any other matter remaining to be dealt with, after an application or appeal is withdrawn`
    `sch 7 an application for a direction or order mentioned in section 40(1) or 58(1)`
    `sch 7 referring an appeal to the Court of Appeal under section 29D(2)(b)`
    `sch 7 deciding under paragraph 2(k)(ii) whether it is appropriate for 2 Judges to hear and decide an appeal`
    `sch 7 the power under section 29D(1) to transfer to the Court of Appeal an appeal made to the Appellate Division`
    `sch 7 the power under section 29E(1) to transfer to the Appellate Division an appeal made to the Court of Appeal`
    `sch 7 an application to vary or discharge a direction or order made by the Registrar, the Deputy Registrar or an Assistant Registrar`
    `sch 7 an application to extend the time for filing and serving a notice of appeal`
    `sch 7 an application to discharge or vary a direction or order made under section 40(1) or 58(1) by a single Judge`
    `sch 7 an application for permission to appeal, to extend time for applying for it, or for a declaration that it is not required`
    `sch 7 an application for a stay of execution or enforcement, or of proceedings under the decision appealed from`
    `sch 7 an appeal against an interlocutory judgment`
    `sch 7 an appeal against a judgment or order obtained after an application for admission under section 15 of the Legal Profession Act 1966`
    `sch 7 an appeal against a judgment or order obtained after an assessment of damages`
    `sch 7 an appeal against a judgment or order obtained after a taking of accounts between parties`
    `sch 7 an appeal against a judgment or order obtained after proceedings other than the trial or hearing of an action or matter commenced by originating process`
    `sch 7 an appeal which under a written law may be heard and decided by 2 Judges`
    `sch 7 any other appeal`
    `sch 7 another matter`

-- The facts that paragraph 2(k) turns on, which the matter itself does not show.
DECLARE `sch 7 para 2(k) facts` HAS
    `all parties consent for the case to be decided by the Appellate Division consisting of 2 Judges` IS A BOOLEAN
    `all parties consent for the case to be decided by the Appellate Division without hearing oral arguments` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the Appellate Division, consisting of at least a single Judge, considers it appropriate for 2 Judges to hear and decide the appeal` IS A BOOLEAN

-- src:6284 | 1.—(1) Despite section 32(1), the following cases may be heard and decided by
-- src:6285 | the Appellate Division consisting of a single Judge or 2 Judges:
-- src:6287 | (a) an application —
-- src:6289 | (i) to record a judgment, or an order, that is made by consent of the
-- src:6290 | parties; or
-- src:6292 | (ii) to make an order that is incidental to any such judgment or
-- src:6293 | order;
-- src:6295 | (b) an application to adduce further evidence in proceedings before the
-- src:6296 | Appellate Division;
-- src:6298 | (c) an application for costs, or any other matter that remains to be dealt
-- src:6299 | with, after an application or appeal to the Appellate Division is
-- src:6300 | withdrawn;
-- src:6302 | (d) an application for any direction or order mentioned in section 40(1).
@ref Seventh Schedule para 1(1) (src:6284-6302)
GIVEN matter IS A `sch 7 matter before the court`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 para 1(1) the matter is one of cases (a) to (d), for a single Judge or 2 Judges of the Appellate Division` MEANS
    CONSIDER matter
    WHEN `sch 7 an application to record a judgment or order made by consent` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an application for an order incidental to a judgment or order made by consent` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an application to adduce further evidence in proceedings before the court` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an application for costs, or any other matter remaining to be dealt with, after an application or appeal is withdrawn` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an application for a direction or order mentioned in section 40(1) or 58(1)` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the following cases may be heard and decided by the Appellate Division consisting of a single Judge or 2 Judges" [handed to a list, and no head in the list holds; delegated-definition ruling (a) of 2026-11-05]
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- src:6304 | (2) Despite section 32(1), the Appellate Division consisting of a single Judge or
-- src:6305 | 2 Judges may —
-- src:6307 | (a) refer an appeal to the Court of Appeal under section 29D(2)(b); and
-- src:6309 | (b) for the purposes of paragraph 2(k)(ii), decide whether it is appropriate
-- src:6310 | for the Appellate Division consisting of 2 Judges to hear and decide an
-- src:6311 | appeal to which that paragraph applies.
@ref Seventh Schedule para 1(2) (src:6304-6311)
GIVEN matter IS A `sch 7 matter before the court`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 para 1(2) the matter is one of the two powers a single Judge or 2 Judges of the Appellate Division may exercise` MEANS
    CONSIDER matter
    WHEN `sch 7 referring an appeal to the Court of Appeal under section 29D(2)(b)` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 deciding under paragraph 2(k)(ii) whether it is appropriate for 2 Judges to hear and decide an appeal` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the Appellate Division consisting of a single Judge or 2 Judges may (a) refer an appeal to the Court of Appeal under section 29D(2)(b); and (b) for the purposes of paragraph 2(k)(ii), decide whether it is appropriate" [handed to a list, and no head in the list holds; delegated-definition ruling (a) of 2026-11-05]
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- src:6313 | (3) Despite section 32(1), the Appellate Division consisting of a single Judge
-- src:6314 | may hear and decide an application to vary or discharge any direction or order
-- src:6315 | made by the Registrar, the Deputy Registrar or an Assistant Registrar exercising
-- src:6316 | any of the powers of the Appellate Division.
@ref Seventh Schedule para 1(3) (src:6313-6316)
GIVEN matter IS A `sch 7 matter before the court`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 para 1(3) the matter is an application to vary or discharge a Registrar's direction, for a single Judge of the Appellate Division` MEANS
    CONSIDER matter
    WHEN `sch 7 an application to vary or discharge a direction or order made by the Registrar, the Deputy Registrar or an Assistant Registrar` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the Appellate Division consisting of a single Judge may hear and decide an application to vary or discharge any direction or order made by the Registrar, the Deputy Registrar or an Assistant Registrar"
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- src:6320 | 2. Despite section 32(1), the following cases may be heard and decided by the
-- src:6321 | Appellate Division consisting of 2 Judges:
-- src:6330 | (a) an application to extend the time for filing and serving a notice of
-- src:6331 | appeal;
-- src:6333 | (b) an application to discharge or vary any direction or order made under
-- src:6334 | section 40(1) by the Appellate Division consisting of a single Judge;
-- src:6336 | (c) an application for permission to appeal to the Appellate Division,
-- src:6337 | whether under this Act or any other written law, an application to
-- src:6338 | extend time for applying for such permission or an application for a
-- src:6339 | declaration that such permission is not required;
-- src:6345 | (d) an application to the Appellate Division for a stay of execution or
-- src:6346 | enforcement (whether pending or after the appeal) or a stay of
-- src:6347 | proceedings under the decision appealed from;
-- src:6351 | (e) an appeal against an interlocutory judgment;
-- src:6353 | (f) an appeal against any judgment or order obtained after the hearing of
-- src:6354 | an application for the admission of a person under section 15 of the
-- src:6355 | Legal Profession Act 1966;
-- src:6357 | (g) an appeal against any judgment or order obtained after the hearing of
-- src:6358 | an assessment of damages;
-- src:6360 | (h) an appeal against any judgment or order obtained after the hearing of a
-- src:6361 | taking of accounts between parties;
-- src:6363 | (i) an appeal against any judgment or order obtained after any
-- src:6364 | proceedings other than the trial or hearing of any action or matter
-- src:6365 | commenced by any originating process;
-- src:6367 | (j) an appeal which, under any written law, may be heard and decided by
-- src:6368 | 2 Judges;
-- src:6370 | (k) any appeal where —
-- src:6372 | (i) all parties consent for the case to be —
-- src:6374 | (A) decided by the Appellate Division consisting of 2 Judges;
-- src:6375 | and
-- src:6377 | (B) decided by the Appellate Division without hearing oral
-- src:6378 | arguments; and
-- src:6380 | (ii) the Appellate Division (consisting of at least a single Judge)
-- src:6381 | considers it appropriate for the Appellate Division consisting
-- src:6382 | of 2 Judges to hear and decide the appeal.
@ref Seventh Schedule para 2 (src:6320-6382)
GIVEN matter IS A `sch 7 matter before the court`
      facts IS A `sch 7 para 2(k) facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 para 2 the matter is one of cases (a) to (k), for 2 Judges of the Appellate Division` MEANS
    CONSIDER matter
    WHEN `sch 7 an application to extend the time for filing and serving a notice of appeal` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an application to discharge or vary a direction or order made under section 40(1) or 58(1) by a single Judge` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an application for permission to appeal, to extend time for applying for it, or for a declaration that it is not required` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an application for a stay of execution or enforcement, or of proceedings under the decision appealed from` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an appeal against an interlocutory judgment` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an appeal against a judgment or order obtained after an application for admission under section 15 of the Legal Profession Act 1966` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an appeal against a judgment or order obtained after an assessment of damages` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an appeal against a judgment or order obtained after a taking of accounts between parties` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an appeal against a judgment or order obtained after proceedings other than the trial or hearing of an action or matter commenced by originating process` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an appeal which under a written law may be heard and decided by 2 Judges` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- (k): any appeal where all parties consent to 2 Judges and to no oral arguments, and the Appellate Division considers it appropriate
    WHEN `sch 7 any other appeal` THEN
        IF facts's `all parties consent for the case to be decided by the Appellate Division consisting of 2 Judges`
           AND facts's `all parties consent for the case to be decided by the Appellate Division without hearing oral arguments`
           AND facts's `the Appellate Division, consisting of at least a single Judge, considers it appropriate for 2 Judges to hear and decide the appeal`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "any appeal where (i) all parties consent for the case to be (A) decided by the Appellate Division consisting of 2 Judges; and (B) decided by the Appellate Division without hearing oral arguments; and (ii) the Appellate Division ... considers it appropriate"
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the following cases may be heard and decided by the Appellate Division consisting of 2 Judges" [handed to a list, and no head in the list holds; delegated-definition ruling (a) of 2026-11-05]
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

