-- src:6426 | powers of the Court of Appeal.
@ref Seventh Schedule para 3(3) (src:6423-6426)
GIVEN matter IS A `sch 7 matter before the court`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 para 3(3) the matter is an application to vary or discharge a Registrar's direction, for a single Judge of the Court of Appeal` MEANS
    CONSIDER matter
    WHEN `sch 7 an application to vary or discharge a direction or order made by the Registrar, the Deputy Registrar or an Assistant Registrar` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the Court of Appeal consisting of a single Judge may hear and decide an application to vary or discharge any direction or order made by the Registrar, the Deputy Registrar or an Assistant Registrar"
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- src:6430 | 4. Despite section 50(1), the following cases may be heard and decided by the
-- src:6431 | Court of Appeal consisting of 2 Judges:
-- src:6433 | (a) an application to extend the time for filing and serving a notice of
-- src:6434 | appeal;
-- src:6436 | (b) an application to discharge or vary any direction or order made under
-- src:6437 | section 58(1) by the Court of Appeal consisting of a single Judge;
-- src:6439 | (c) an application for permission to appeal to the Court of Appeal, whether
-- src:6440 | under this Act or any other written law, an application to extend time
-- src:6449 | for applying for such permission or an application for a declaration that
-- src:6450 | such permission is not required;
-- src:6456 | (d) an application to the Court of Appeal for a stay of execution or
-- src:6457 | enforcement (whether pending or after the appeal) or a stay of
-- src:6458 | proceedings under the decision appealed from;
-- src:6462 | (e) an appeal against an interlocutory judgment;
-- src:6464 | (f) an appeal against any judgment or order obtained after the hearing of
-- src:6465 | an application for the admission of a person under section 15 of the
-- src:6466 | Legal Profession Act 1966;
-- src:6468 | (g) an appeal against any judgment or order obtained after the hearing of
-- src:6469 | an assessment of damages;
-- src:6471 | (h) an appeal against any judgment or order obtained after the hearing of a
-- src:6472 | taking of accounts between parties;
-- src:6474 | (i) an appeal against any judgment or order obtained after any
-- src:6475 | proceedings other than the trial or hearing of any action or matter
-- src:6476 | commenced by any originating process;
-- src:6478 | (j) an appeal which, under any written law, may be heard and decided by
-- src:6479 | 2 Judges.
@ref Seventh Schedule para 4 (src:6430-6479)
GIVEN matter IS A `sch 7 matter before the court`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 para 4 the matter is one of cases (a) to (j), for 2 Judges of the Court of Appeal` MEANS
    CONSIDER matter
    WHEN `sch 7 an application to extend the time for filing and serving a notice of appeal` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an application to discharge or vary a direction or order made under section 40(1) or 58(1) by a single Judge` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an application for permission to appeal, to extend time for applying for it, or for a declaration that it is not required` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an application for a stay of execution or enforcement, or of proceedings under the decision appealed from` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an appeal against an interlocutory judgment` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an appeal against a judgment or order obtained after an application for admission under section 15 of the Legal Profession Act 1966` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an appeal against a judgment or order obtained after an assessment of damages` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an appeal against a judgment or order obtained after a taking of accounts between parties` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an appeal against a judgment or order obtained after proceedings other than the trial or hearing of an action or matter commenced by originating process` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `sch 7 an appeal which under a written law may be heard and decided by 2 Judges` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the following cases may be heard and decided by the Court of Appeal consisting of 2 Judges" [handed to a list, and no head in the list holds; delegated-definition ruling (a) of 2026-11-05]
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- The Appellate Division: paragraphs 1 and 2.  A single Judge alone is allowed only for paragraph 1(3); "a single Judge or 2 Judges" for 1(1) and 1(2); 2 Judges for paragraph 2.
GIVEN matter IS A `sch 7 matter before the court`
      facts IS A `sch 7 para 2(k) facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Smaller panel`
`sch 7 paras 1 and 2 the smaller panel the Appellate Division may sit as` MEANS
    IF `sch 7 para 1(3) the matter is an application to vary or discharge a Registrar's direction, for a single Judge of the Appellate Division` matter EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN JUST `a single Judge`
    ELSE IF `sch 7 para 1(1) the matter is one of cases (a) to (d), for a single Judge or 2 Judges of the Appellate Division` matter EQUALS JUST TRUE
            OR `sch 7 para 1(2) the matter is one of the two powers a single Judge or 2 Judges of the Appellate Division may exercise` matter EQUALS JUST TRUE
         THEN JUST `a single Judge or 2 Judges`
         ELSE IF `sch 7 para 2 the matter is one of cases (a) to (k), for 2 Judges of the Appellate Division` matter facts EQUALS JUST TRUE
              THEN JUST `2 Judges`
              -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the Schedule does not list the matter, and s 36(1) allows fewer than 3 Judges only "if the Seventh Schedule so provides"
              ELSE NOTHING

-- The Court of Appeal: paragraphs 3 and 4.
GIVEN matter IS A `sch 7 matter before the court`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Smaller panel`
`sch 7 paras 3 and 4 the smaller panel the Court of Appeal may sit as` MEANS
    IF `sch 7 para 3(3) the matter is an application to vary or discharge a Registrar's direction, for a single Judge of the Court of Appeal` matter EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN JUST `a single Judge`
    ELSE IF `sch 7 para 3(1) the matter is one of cases (a) to (d), for a single Judge or 2 Judges of the Court of Appeal` matter EQUALS JUST TRUE
            OR `sch 7 para 3(2) the matter is one of the two transfer powers a single Judge or 2 Judges of the Court of Appeal may exercise` matter EQUALS JUST TRUE
         THEN JUST `a single Judge or 2 Judges`
         ELSE IF `sch 7 para 4 the matter is one of cases (a) to (j), for 2 Judges of the Court of Appeal` matter EQUALS JUST TRUE
              THEN JUST `2 Judges`
              -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the Schedule does not list the matter, and s 54(1) allows fewer than 3 Judges only "if the Seventh Schedule so provides"
              ELSE NOTHING

-- src:6277 | CASES WHERE CIVIL JURISDICTION
-- src:6278 | OF APPELLATE DIVISION OR COURT OF APPEAL
-- src:6279 | CAN BE EXERCISED BY LESS THAN 3 JUDGES
@ref Seventh Schedule (heading) (src:6277-6279)
-- Seam 14 of PLAN.md.  The Schedule speaks of the Appellate Division and the Court of Appeal only; for any other court the answer is NOTHING.
GIVEN court IS A Court
      matter IS A `sch 7 matter before the court`
      facts IS A `sch 7 para 2(k) facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Smaller panel`
`sch 7 the smaller panel the Seventh Schedule allows` MEANS
    CONSIDER court
    WHEN `Appellate Division` THEN `sch 7 paras 1 and 2 the smaller panel the Appellate Division may sit as` matter facts
    WHEN `Court of Appeal` THEN `sch 7 paras 3 and 4 the smaller panel the Court of Appeal may sit as` matter
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the Schedule is headed "Cases where civil jurisdiction of Appellate Division or Court of Appeal can be exercised by less than 3 Judges"
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- src:6277 | CASES WHERE CIVIL JURISDICTION
-- src:6278 | OF APPELLATE DIVISION OR COURT OF APPEAL
-- src:6279 | CAN BE EXERCISED BY LESS THAN 3 JUDGES
@ref Seventh Schedule (heading) (src:6277-6279)
-- The yes/no twin of seam 14 (assumed, not ruled: a result that is a panel cannot carry JUST FALSE).
-- JUST TRUE: the Schedule lets fewer than 3 Judges hear the matter.  JUST FALSE: the Schedule does not list it, in the two courts it speaks of.
GIVEN court IS A Court
      matter IS A `sch 7 matter before the court`
      facts IS A `sch 7 para 2(k) facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 the Seventh Schedule allows fewer than 3 Judges` MEANS
    CONSIDER court
    WHEN `Appellate Division` THEN
        CONSIDER `sch 7 the smaller panel the Seventh Schedule allows` court matter facts
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if the Seventh Schedule so provides" [handed to a list, and no head in the list holds; delegated-definition ruling (a) of 2026-11-05]
        WHEN NOTHING THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE
    WHEN `Court of Appeal` THEN
        CONSIDER `sch 7 the smaller panel the Seventh Schedule allows` court matter facts
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if the Seventh Schedule so provides" [handed to a list, and no head in the list holds; delegated-definition ruling (a) of 2026-11-05]
        WHEN NOTHING THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the Schedule speaks of the Appellate Division and the Court of Appeal only
    OTHERWISE NOTHING
